    "md5": "704dad662e1e76d4a95ff45adccd6dd1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1860,
    "chunk_text": "The continued occupation of civilian lands and the prolonged military presence in civilian areas delay normal civil administration and it undermines confidence-building in the reconciliation process. The release of occupied lands and demilitarization of the Northern and Eastern Provinces with military deployment progressively reduced to levels comparable with the rest of Sri Lanka, except where genuine national security considerations require otherwise, are essential for reconciliation, resettlement and economic recovery.\n\nWhile I welcome the release of lands occupied by two military camps in Batticaloa following representations I made at the Ministerial Consultative Committee on Defence, which was chaired by His Excellency the President as Minister of Defence, I must also say that many affected communities continue to protest over the slow pace of land releases.\n\nSir, I must acknowledge that the President was able to release lands held by two military camps in Batticaloa. We are very thankful to him for releasing those lands. After we made representations at the Ministerial Consultative Committee on Defence, those lands occupied by both the Murakkoddanchenai and the\n\n241 242\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db8\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nParuthithurai Camps were released by the President. We acknowledge it and thank him for that. However, Sir, still, there are various protests happening across the North and the East pertaining to land release.\n\nWill the Hon. Minister inform this House -\n\n(a) the total extent of private and State lands occupied by the armed forces in the Northern and Eastern Provinces, district-wise, the extent, locations and dates of lands released since the present Government assumed office, the extent of lands that remain under military occupation and the Government\u2019s timeline for releasing the remaining lands?"
  },
